e, G ∼ = k1 P4 ∪ k2 P8\nwhere k1 , k2 ≥ 0 and k1 + k2 ≥ 1. Since γtg (P4 ) = γtg\u0013 (P4 ) = 3 and γtg (P8 ) =\nγtg\u0013 (P8 ) = 6, the optimal strategy of Staller is whenever Dominator starts playing\non a component of G, she plays on that component and adopts her optimal strategy\nMy Favorite Domination Game Conjectures 141\n\non the component. This shows that γtg (G) = 3k1 + 6k2 = 3n/4, where n = 4k1 +\n8k2 is the number of vertices in G.\nWe discuss next special cases where the 34 -Game Total Domination Conjecture\nhas been proven. Together with my distinguished Hungarian coauthors, Csilla Bujtás\nand Zsolt Tuza, we proved in [9] that the conjecture holds over the class of graphs\nwith minimum degree at least 2. To do this, we raise the problem to a higher level\nby introducing a transversal game in hypergraphs, and establish a tight upper bound\non the game transversal number of a hypergraph with all edges of size at least 2 in\nterms of its order and size. As an application of this result, we prove that if G is a\ngraph of order n with minimum degree at least 2, then γtg (G) ≤ 11 8\nn.\nTheorem 10 ([9]) If G is a graph of order n with δ(G) ≥ 2, then γtg (G) ≤ 8\n11 n.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nTeresa W. Haynes, Stephen T. Hedetniemi, Michael A. Henning - Domination in Graphs - Core Concepts-Springer (2023) | PDF | Theoretical Computer Science | Combinatorics (https://www.scribd.com/document/927553953/Teresa-W-Haynes-Stephen-T-Hedetniemi-Michael-a-Henning-Domination-in-Graphs-Core-Concepts-Springer-2023)\nciteturn6search4 [wordlim: 200] Published: 3.8 years ago; Crawled: 6 months ago; a second NP-completeness proof to the following decision problem. ... Recall that we abbreviate a total dominating ... NP-Completeness of Domination Parameters 55\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nPapers from 2026-10-02 - arXiv Combinatorics (https://www.arxiv.symmetri